N2  - Background: Several patients have secondary telangiectasias (TAE) on the inner thigh after endovenous thermal ablation of the great saphenous vein (GSV). We found no work directly devoted to this issue in the literature review, except individual clinical cases.This study aimed to determine the percentage of secondary TAEs after endovenous laser ablation (EVLA) of GSV and identify the risk factors for their development. Methods: Patients who underwent isolated EVLA of GSV without simultaneous elimination of the tributaries and/or perforating veins were sequentially included in a prospective non-comparative single-center study. Patients were invited the next day and 3 months after the procedure, when, in addition to visual and ultrasound examination, repeated photo documentation of the lower extremities was also carried out.Then, three independent surgeons with at least 5 years of experience separately conducted a comparative analysis of photographs before and 3 months after EVLA and evaluated the results on a four-point scale, where 0 is the absence of new TAEs, 1 is the appearance of an insignificant number of TAEs, 2 is new TAEs in moderate amount, and 3 is the large number of new TAEs. The benchmarks for this assessment were photographs of the extremities of patients who had previously received treatment before this study. Results: One-hundred-twenty-three limbs in 103 patients were evaluated. The appearance of secondary TAEs on the inner thigh was recorded on 26 (21.1%, 95% CI [15%, 29%]) limbs. In 17.9% of cases, these were TAEs in an insignificant amount, while their moderate or large amount was found in 2.4% and 0.8% of the extremities, respectively. There was complete agreement between the three doctors (Fleis's kappa = 0.92; P < 0.0001).The median age in the group without new TAEs was 40 years (95% CI [34; 41]), compared to 52.5 years in the group with secondary TAEs (95% CI [46; 57.5]) (P = 0.006). Differences in gender, body mass index, CEAP, VCSS, GSV diameter 3 cm below the saphenofemoral junction, and in the middle thigh were not statistically significant. Conclusion: Secondary TAEs along the inner thigh after EVLA of GSV develop in 21.1% of cases (17.9% in insignificant amounts, moderate in 2.4%, and large in 0.8%). Older age is a risk factor for their development.
