N2  - Background: Adhesive small bowel obstruction (ASBO) is one of the most common emergency surgical conditions requiring hospitalisation. Non-operative management with 'drip and suck' is often sufficient in the majority of patients without signs of peritonitis/ strangulation, but surgery is still required in 30% of cases. Operative management is associated with significant morbidity and prolonged hospital stay. Our previous study demonstrated that electroacupuncture (EA) reduces the duration of postoperative ileus and hospital stay after laparoscopic colorectal surgery [1], and we hypothesise that EA is also effective in treating uncomplicated ASBO. Objectives and research questions: To compare the efficacy of EA and sham acupuncture (SA) in accelerating resolution of bowel obstruction and reducing the operative rate in patients with uncomplicated ASBO. Methods: Design: Prospective, randomised, controlled, superiority trial with 2 arms (1) EA; and (2) SA. Inclusion criteria: Patients diagnosed with ASBO (clinical and radiological evidence; history of abdominal surgery >4 weeks ago). Exclusion criteria: Patients with signs of peritonitis/strangulation, those with suspected intra-abdominal malignancy, those with active inflammatory bowel disease, or those with previous abdominal radiotherapy. Primary outcome parameter: operative rate. Secondary outcome parameters: time to resolution of bowel obstruction, length of hospital stay, time to surgery, bowel resection rate, and morbidity. Group size calculation: Assuming a reduction of operative rate from 30% in the SA group to 10% in the EA group (based on previous studies), type I error: 5%, power: 80%, attrition rate: 10%, we need 68 patients per group.
