N2  - Objectives: In order to mobilize patients surgically treated early, we developed a protocol for accelerated surgical recovery (PROSM), whose main actions are: abbreviation of preoperative fasting, rapid awakening of anesthesia, rigorous pain control, and respiratory physiotherapy associated with ultra early ambulation. In this way, we evaluated the feasibility and safety of the application of the accelerated PROSM protocol in patients undergoing thoracic surgical procedures. Evaluation of secondary outcomes: reduction of hospitalization cost and reduction of hospitalization period compared to records of patients submitted to thoracic surgical procedures with the same profile obtained in our database. Methods: This is a prospective cohort study, with 51 patients submitted to elective pulmonary resections surgeries according to the PROSM guidelines. Participants were assessed pre and post-operative through the Visual Analogue Scale (EVA)to classify the degree of pain and to classify functional independence through the Functional Independence Measure (MIF)Scale. Physical therapy interventions such as breathing exercises, upper and lower limb exercises, and ultra-early ambulation were performed in all patients. For pain control, we use the appliance of transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)on acupuncture points that promote immediate analgesic effects on the chest wall. The acupuncture points involved were Huatuojiaji of T9 T2 and Dingchuan. Results: The success of PROSM depends on three factors. The first is the first physiotherapeutic exercises. The second is early feeding. The third is pain control, avoiding the use of opioids because of its side effects. The results showed that there was a significant difference in pain (p < 0.0001)while there was no significant statistical difference in the classification of functional independence (p > 0.05)of the participants. We also observed that physiotherapy brought benefits such as early withdrawal of the thoracic drainage and fewer days of hospitalization. Conclusion: It is concluded that the application of an Accelerated Recovery Protocol (PROSM)is feasible within our reality, which is easy to apply, low cost and low risk to patients and professionals. Furthermore, the physiotherapist's performance is fundamental to improve morbidity and shorten hospital stay. Disclosure of Interest: None declared.
