N2  - Electro-acupuncture (EA) is commonly used as an alternative treatment for gallstone disease. This study aimed to investigate the effectiveness of EA. If shown effective, patients could preserve their gallbladders from cholecystectomy. Within a treatment period of 10 weeks, 132 subjects with symptomatic gallstone diseases (size ≤ 8 mm) were randomly allocated into either treatment group (n = 66), or control group (n = 66). Treatment group was offered 20 EA sessions while the control group were clinically observed. The primary outcome was the proportion of patients with total/partial clearance of gallstone confirmed by ultrasonography between the two groups. Secondary outcomes were the evaluation of Patients Reported Outcomes (PROs) (e.g.SF6D, GIQLI) collected throughout the treatment period. By February 2018, 46 subjects had completed the study (treatment = 22, control = 24). From treatment group, 2 (vs 1 in control group) subjects showed total clearance while 4 (vs 2 in control group) subjects showed partial clearance. Clearance and ejection fraction change differences between the two groups were not significant (p > 0.05). No severe adverse event was reported. PROs have shown improvement from both groups but it was not statistically significant (p > 0.05). The data collected are encouraging and the use of EA for treating gallstone was found to be safe despite the study is still on-going. Because of the small completed cases sample size, it was difficult to conclude the effectiveness of EA for gallstone diseases at present stage. However, conducting a controlled clinical trial in EA for gallstone diseases was feasible and the safety could be assured.
