N2  - Background and Aims: Caesarean section (CS) is common surgery with a transient impairment in gastrointestinal (GI) motility, which can lead delayed hospital discharge. Transcutaneous electrical acustimulation (TEA) was reported to improve postoperative recovery after surgical removal of GI cancers. However, it is unclear whether TEA is also effective in improving postoperative recovery after commonly performed non-GI surgeries. The aim of the study was thus to explore the effect and mechanisms of TEA on postoperative recovery after CS. Methods: A total of 108 women (age 30.2±4.6) who underwent CS were randomized to receive TEA (n=54) or sham-TEA (n=54). Four hours after CS, electrogastrogram (EGG) and electrocardiogram (ECG) were recorded for 30 min to assess gastric slow waves and autonomic functions, respectively. TEA at ST36 or sham-TEA at non-acupoints was performed for 1 hour 4.5 h after surgery and then twice daily from postoperative days (POD) 1 to 3. In the morning of POD4, the EGG and ECG were recorded again for 30 min. The measurements included time of first flatus, time of first defecation, time of resuming semi-fluids, the number of spontaneous bowel movements (SBMs) within 4 POD and GI symptoms. Results: 1) TEA enhanced postoperative recovery associated with lower GI motility (see Table 1), reflected as a reduction in time of first flatus (P=0.002) and time of first defecation (P<0.001), an increase in the Bristol stool score (P<0.001) and the number of SBMs (P<0.001) in comparison with sham-TEA. TEA also reduced the percentage of patients who experienced bloating (13% vs 35%, P=0.013, vs. sham-TEA), straining during defecation (28% vs 48%, P=0.037), sensation of incomplete evacuation (18% vs 44%, P=0.007) and sensation of anorectal blockage during defecation (22% vs 44%, P=0.025). 2) TEA reduced symptoms associated with upper GI motility (Table 1), including a reduction in time to resume semifluid (P=0.008 vs sham-TEA), and the percentage of patients who reported loss of appetite (50% vs 74.1%, P=0.017), belching (9.3% vs 29.6%, P=0.015). Physiologically, TEA but not sham-TEA increased the percentage of normal gastric slow waves on POD4 compared with POD0 (63.1±12.1% vs 53.2±13.8%, P=0.001). 3) TEA reduced the pain score from POD1 to POD3 (P<0.001, see Table 1). 4) TEA but not sham-TEA increased vagal activity (0.44±0.15 vs 0.38±0.14, P=0.013) and decreased sympathetic activity (0.56 ±0.15 vs 0.62±0.14, P=0.013) on POD4 compared with POD0. 5) Three factors were found to be independent predictors of shortened time for the first defecation: the use of TEA, a shorter surgical duration, and a lower sympathetic activity on POD4. Conclusions: Needless non-invasive TEA is effective in promoting postoperative recovery of symptoms associated with both upper and lower GI motility by enhancing vagal and suppressing sympathetic activities. Primary Outcome Measures in patients with TEA and Sham-TEA [Table Presented]
